1. Common User Tier: Allows the public to view the system or website purely in Read-Only mode. Dynamic data processing, file writes, and database modifications are physically disabled, keeping critical information visible to the population during a crisis without exposing the server to tampering.
2. Administrator Tier ("One-Shot Ultimatum"): The legitimate administrator consumes the last certificate generated by an Edge Replicator to perform emergency logins. The system grants a single, one-time open session.
3. Firmware Quarantining: The exact millisecond the admin session terminates, the chip's firmware enters a forced lockdown state. Even if the global internet is restored minutes later and the central Interlocutor Ring sends valid certificates, the device rejects them.
4. Coercion Hardening (No Logical Backdoors): The protocol explicitly forbids master digital bypass keys. To return the server to an active operational state, the owner must be physically present at the machine chassis and perform a hard local firmware flash using a factory-signed hardware token. This enforces physical auditing, completely neutralizing remote extortion, kidnapping, or physical duress vectors.

5. SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS & OPEN STANDARDS

To ensure that the physical fusing mechanics (eFuses) and firmware quarantines cannot be abused as logical or electromagnetic backdoors by state-sponsored actors, AIP enforces the following compliance frameworks:
• Open-Hardware Specification: The silicon wafer layout, transistor pathways, and logical clock circuitry must be entirely open-source, allowing independent microscopic laboratory audits against hidden parasitic gates.
• Post-Quantum Contextual Cryptography: All ring and replicator signatures utilize lattice-based cryptographic algorithms to resist decryption by quantum computing architectures, ensuring the absolute integrity of the metadata envelope for decades to come.

