### 🌐 Trust Protocol Execution Comparison: Legacy HTTPS (Blind Certificates) vs. Our AIP Model


### ❌ LEGACY HTTPS PROTOCOL (30-YEAR-OLD BLIND CERTIFICATES)

• ⚡ Validation Vulnerability: STATIC TARGET & CONTEXTUALLY BLINDCertificates issued by traditional Certificate Authorities (CAs) only attest that a domain name belongs to an entity. They carry a static cryptographic key valid for months, remaining completely blind to the actual software state running inside the server. They have zero visibility into the application's "DNA."
• 🔒 Integrity Vulnerability: EXPOSURE TO APPLICATION EXPLOITS (PHP-to-Shell)If a hospital's or a government server's PHP stack is compromised via a zero-day remote code execution (RCE) exploit, HTTPS continues to display the trusted "green padlock" in the user's browser. It blindly authenticates a secure, encrypted pipe directly to a hijacked machine spewing malware, actively deceiving the victims.
• 🌍 Network Resiliency: CATASTROPHIC INFRASTRUCTURE OR BLACKOUT COLLAPSERelies entirely on real-time online revocation servers (OCSP/CRLs). If regional internet routing fails or a continental blackout occurs, client machines cannot verify if a certificate has been compromised. The protocol either fails open (vulnerable) or renders the air-gapped system completely bricked.

